WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.776
A payments company rents a computer in the cloud to run its database.

2
00:00:06.776 --> 00:00:11.503
That computer comes with its own disk, and the day's payments are written there.

3
00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:15.933
Then the computer fails, or the cloud moves it to another machine.

4
00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:17.355
Its disk goes with it.

5
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:21.209
So where should a database keep the payments it must never lose?

6
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.287
Picture the café kitchen.

7
00:00:23.287 --> 00:00:25.318
Its shelf holds the ingredients.

8
00:00:25.318 --> 00:00:28.491
If the kitchen burns down, the shelf goes with it.

9
00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:31.284
That's the rented computer and its own disk.

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.342
So keep the stock in a warehouse down the road.

11
00:00:34.342 --> 00:00:36.519
A fire in the kitchen loses nothing.

12
00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:38.696
In the cloud, that's a network disk.

13
00:00:38.696 --> 00:00:42.385
It lives on the provider's storage machines, and it survives.

14
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.682
The catch: every fetch is now a trip down the road.

15
00:00:45.682 --> 00:00:48.553
Every read or write is a call over the network.

16
00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:51.216
And a database makes a lot of trips.

17
00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:53.053
It saves each change twice.

18
00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:59.244
First a short note at the end of its log, a file it only adds to, so a crash loses nothing.

19
00:00:59.244 --> 00:01:04.687
Later, the changed pages, the blocks its tables live in, often 8 kilobytes each.

20
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:11.259
Now keep a mirror in a second availability zone, a separate building with its own power and network.

21
00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:12.849
Every trip happens twice.

22
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.712
Amazon counted.

23
00:01:14.712 --> 00:01:23.281
A mirrored MySQL database, a popular free one, needed 7.4 reads or writes to storage for each transaction.

24
00:01:23.281 --> 00:01:25.787
Each of those is called an I/O.

25
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:28.555
Wouldn't it be nice to send just the short note?

26
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.281
Back to the kitchen.

27
00:01:30.281 --> 00:01:33.776
The cook sends the warehouse only a slip: used two eggs.

28
00:01:33.776 --> 00:01:37.832
The warehouse clerks update their own stock pages from the slips.

29
00:01:37.832 --> 00:01:41.327
The kitchen keeps only today's ingredients on its shelf.

30
00:01:41.533 --> 00:01:42.259
Now map it.

31
00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:47.671
The kitchen is the database instance, the computer doing the work, called compute.

32
00:01:47.671 --> 00:01:50.641
The warehouse is a fleet of storage machines.

33
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.332
The slip is a redo log record, a short note saying what a change did.

34
00:01:55.332 --> 00:02:02.163
The storage machines build the 8 kilobyte pages themselves, so pages never cross the network on a write.

35
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:06.448
And the shelf is only a cache, a quick copy that can be refilled.

36
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:10.102
This is called separating storage from compute.

37
00:02:10.102 --> 00:02:17.411
Amazon's Aurora, a cloud database built from MySQL, sums it up in one line: the log is the database.

38
00:02:17.633 --> 00:02:22.319
Same 30-minute test, same size of machine.

39
00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:28.454
Mirrored MySQL: 780,000 transactions, at 7.4 I/Os each.

40
00:02:28.454 --> 00:02:32.693
Aurora: 27 million, at 0.95 I/Os each.

41
00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:38.056
That's 35 times the transactions, with 7.7 times fewer I/Os each.

42
00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:41.729
And Aurora was keeping six copies of the data.

43
00:02:41.729 --> 00:02:44.044
More breakfasts, fewer trips.

44
00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:45.748
Six copies, kept how?

45
00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:49.062
Aurora cuts the data into 10-gigabyte segments.

46
00:02:49.062 --> 00:02:53.012
Each segment has six copies, two in each of three zones.

47
00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.766
A write counts once four copies have it.

48
00:02:55.766 --> 00:02:56.984
A read needs three.

49
00:02:56.984 --> 00:03:02.244
Those numbers are quorums: how many copies must answer before an operation counts.

50
00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:04.081
Now a whole zone fails.

51
00:03:04.081 --> 00:03:09.096
Its power, its network, or a bad change that hits every machine in it.

52
00:03:09.096 --> 00:03:10.242
Two copies gone.

53
00:03:10.242 --> 00:03:11.603
Can we still write?

54
00:03:11.603 --> 00:03:12.749
Pause and guess.

55
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:16.278
Yes.

56
00:03:16.278 --> 00:03:19.777
Four copies are left, and a write needs four.

57
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.331
Then, while that zone is down, a disk in zone c dies.

58
00:03:24.331 --> 00:03:25.230
Three left.

59
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.211
Writes stop.

60
00:03:26.211 --> 00:03:27.600
Reads still work.

61
00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.469
Did we lose a saved write?

62
00:03:29.469 --> 00:03:29.666
No.

63
00:03:29.666 --> 00:03:32.155
Every saved write reached four copies.

64
00:03:32.155 --> 00:03:38.311
Any three copies include at least one of those four, because three plus four is more than six.

65
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.689
Meanwhile, the storage fleet copies the segment onto a new machine.

66
00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:44.939
Ten gigabytes take about 10 seconds.

67
00:03:44.939 --> 00:03:47.065
That's why the segments are small.

68
00:03:47.065 --> 00:03:50.879
Aurora can't stop disks failing, but it can repair them fast.

69
00:03:51.067 --> 00:03:54.097
The price is a network trip on every write.

70
00:03:54.097 --> 00:03:55.859
Newer designs go further.

71
00:03:55.859 --> 00:04:05.163
They keep the durable copy in object storage, cheap file storage like Amazon S3, to save on fees for bytes that cross between zones.

72
00:04:05.163 --> 00:04:07.136
They pay with slower writes.

73
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.359
So where should the payments live?

74
00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:11.417
Not on a computer that can vanish.

75
00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.384
In the warehouse: six copies, in three buildings.

76
00:04:14.384 --> 00:04:16.564
The kitchen's shelf is only a cache.

77
00:04:16.767 --> 00:04:20.184
Where is the durable copy, and what is only a cache?

78
00:04:20.184 --> 00:04:22.156
Ask that first, of any system.

79
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:23.957
One quick check.

80
00:04:23.957 --> 00:04:28.086
The database instance crashes: the kitchen burns down.

81
00:04:28.086 --> 00:04:29.003
What's lost?

82
00:04:29.003 --> 00:04:30.227
Pause and think.

83
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:34.926
Only the shelf, a cache.

84
00:04:34.926 --> 00:04:37.353
Every saved change is in the warehouse.

85
00:04:37.353 --> 00:04:40.152
A new instance starts, and refills its shelf.
